# Bluetooth LE

## Overview

The Bluetooth radio shares the WCN3680 chip with Wi-Fi. The kernel talks
to it and exposes it as `hci0`; from there
[BlueHeron](https://github.com/mlainez/blue_heron), a Bluetooth LE stack
written in Elixir, drives it directly. There is no BlueZ daemon in
between: BlueHeron sends the raw HCI commands itself.

| Layer | What it does |
|---|---|
| WCN3680 | The radio |
| `btqcomsmd` (kernel) | Carries HCI packets to the chip, exposes `hci0` |
| `BlueHeron.HCI.Transport.HCISocket` | Opens `hci0` for exclusive use |
| `BlueHeron` | Scanning, advertising, GATT services |

This notebook covers Bluetooth **LE** (low energy): beacons, sensors,
fitness trackers, phone apps. Classic Bluetooth (headphones, call
headsets, serial adapters) isn't available: the chip supports it, but
BlueHeron only speaks Bluetooth LE.

Install a BLE scanner on your own phone to follow along, for example
**nRF Connect** (Android and iOS).

## Is the radio ready?

BlueHeron starts with the firmware and sets the controller up in the
background:

```elixir
BlueHeron.HCI.Transport.setup_complete?()
```

```elixir
{:ok, address} = BlueHeron.HCI.Transport.get_setup_param(:bd_addr)
to_string(address)
```

## Scan for nearby devices

Scanning means listening for advertisements: short packets that BLE
devices broadcast a few times per second. BlueHeron forwards every HCI
event to the processes that subscribe to it.

```elixir
alias BlueHeron.HCI.Command.LEController
alias BlueHeron.HCI.Event.LEMeta.AdvertisingReport

BlueHeron.Registry.subscribe()

{:ok, _} = BlueHeron.HCI.Transport.send_hci(%LEController.SetScanParameters{le_scan_type: 0x01})
{:ok, _} = BlueHeron.HCI.Transport.send_hci(%LEController.SetScanEnable{le_scan_enable: true})

collect = fn collect, found, deadline ->
  wait = max(deadline - System.monotonic_time(:millisecond), 0)

  receive do
    {:HCI_EVENT_PACKET, %AdvertisingReport{devices: devices}} ->
      found =
        Enum.reduce(devices, found, fn device, found ->
          name =
            Enum.find_value(device.data, fn
              {"Complete Local Name", name} -> name
              {"Shortened Local Name", name} -> name
              _ -> nil
            end)

          # The signal strength arrives as one byte; read it as a signed number.
          <<rssi::signed>> = <<device.rss>>

          Map.update(found, device.address, %{name: name, rssi: rssi}, fn seen ->
            %{name: seen.name || name, rssi: max(seen.rssi, rssi)}
          end)
        end)

      collect.(collect, found, deadline)
  after
    wait -> found
  end
end

# Listen for ten seconds.
found = collect.(collect, %{}, System.monotonic_time(:millisecond) + 10_000)

{:ok, _} = BlueHeron.HCI.Transport.send_hci(%LEController.SetScanEnable{le_scan_enable: false})

for {address, %{name: name, rssi: rssi}} <- Enum.sort_by(found, fn {_, d} -> -d.rssi end) do
  %{
    address: BlueHeron.Address.parse(address) |> to_string(),
    name: name || "",
    signal: "#{rssi} dBm"
  }
end
|> Kino.DataTable.new()
```

`signal` is how strongly each device was heard: around -40 dBm is right
next to the phone, -90 dBm is at the edge of range. Many devices don't
advertise a name.

## Advertise the phone

Now the Fairphone broadcasts its own advertisements. Look for
**Fairphone-Nerves** in the scanner app on your phone.

```elixir
alias BlueHeron.AdvertisingData

flags = AdvertisingData.flags(le_general_discoverable_mode: true, br_edr_not_supported: true)
name = AdvertisingData.complete_name("Fairphone-Nerves")

BlueHeron.Broadcaster.stop_advertising()
BlueHeron.Broadcaster.set_advertising_data(flags <> name)
BlueHeron.Broadcaster.start_advertising()
```

## Share the battery level

A GATT service is what other devices read once they connect. Bluetooth
defines a standard **Battery Service** (`0x180F`) with a **Battery Level**
characteristic (`0x2A19`), one byte from 0 to 100. Scanner apps know it
and show it as a percentage.

```elixir
alias BlueHeron.GATT.{Characteristic, Service}

battery_level = fn ->
  "/sys/class/power_supply/qg-battery/capacity"
  |> File.read!()
  |> String.trim()
  |> String.to_integer()
end

gap =
  Service.new(%{
    id: :gap,
    type: 0x1800,
    characteristics: [
      Characteristic.new(%{id: {:gap, :device_name}, type: 0x2A00, properties: 0b0000010}),
      Characteristic.new(%{id: {:gap, :appearance}, type: 0x2A01, properties: 0b0000010})
    ],
    read: fn
      {:gap, :device_name} -> "Fairphone-Nerves"
      # Appearance 0x0040: generic phone
      {:gap, :appearance} -> <<0x0040::little-16>>
    end
  })

gatt =
  Service.new(%{
    id: :gatt,
    type: 0x1801,
    characteristics: [
      Characteristic.new(%{id: {:gatt, :service_changed}, type: 0x2A05, properties: 0b0100000})
    ]
  })

battery =
  Service.new(%{
    id: :battery,
    type: 0x180F,
    characteristics: [
      Characteristic.new(%{id: {:battery, :level}, type: 0x2A19, properties: 0b0000010})
    ],
    read: fn {:battery, :level} -> <<battery_level.()>> end
  })

BlueHeron.Peripheral.add_service(gap)
BlueHeron.Peripheral.add_service(gatt)
BlueHeron.Peripheral.add_service(battery)
```

In nRF Connect, connect to **Fairphone-Nerves**, open **Battery Service**
and read **Battery Level**: it matches the battery notebook.

`properties: 0b0000010` marks a characteristic as readable. Write your
own service the same way: pick a random 128-bit UUID for `type`, return
any binary from `read`, and add a `write` function to receive data from
the other phone.

## Pair with a laptop

The Fairphone can also pair like any Bluetooth accessory. It uses "Just
Works" pairing, so there is no code to type.

With the battery service registered (the cell above) and the phone
advertising, open your laptop's Bluetooth settings and set up
**Fairphone-Nerves**. On Linux with GNOME, the phone's battery then
shows up under **Settings → Power**, or with `upower -d` in a terminal.

Services only live until the phone reboots: after a reboot, run the
battery cell again before connecting. If an earlier attempt left the
laptop stuck on "not set up", remove the device on the laptop first.

## Stop advertising

```elixir
BlueHeron.Broadcaster.stop_advertising()
```
